<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Qt's own template, with three deliberate differences. Compare against
    <qt-android>/src/android/templates/AndroidManifest.xml
  after a Qt bump.

  1. NO "%%INSERT_PERMISSIONS" MARKER, and that is the whole point of this file
     existing. androiddeployqt does not read the code to decide what an app may
     do; it unions the permissions declared by every Qt module it deploys and
     pastes them in at that marker. Qt Network is linked transitively by Qt
     Quick, so a calculator that has never opened a socket was asking for
     INTERNET, ACCESS_NETWORK_STATE, READ_EXTERNAL_STORAGE and
     WRITE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE - measured on the first APK this project ever
     built. Gert, 2026sep06: "Remember to request the required permissions for
     the APK, but make them be requested to the system as they become required.
     For example, only if the user desires to change to a folder outside the
     sandbox to integrate Dropbox, and so on." With the marker gone the union is
     pasted nowhere and the app ships asking for nothing at all.

     ONE PERMISSION IS DECLARED, and it is the one Gert's example named.
     MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE is what lets Agape48 keep the calculators in a
     folder of his own - Documents/Agape48Emulator, which his sync client
     watches - instead of one only Agape48 can see. Declaring it asks for
     nothing: it is a "special" permission that no prompt can grant, only a
     switch on a system settings screen that the user reaches deliberately, from
     a button that appears in Settings only after a folder that needs it has
     been chosen. Everything the app does by itself - the folder it starts in,
     and the Android/media folder the "Move it where other apps can see it"
     button hands out - is the app's own storage and needs no permission at all,
     which is why this is not asked for at startup and never will be.

     The Storage Access Framework was the alternative and it could not do the
     job: a content:// tree has no path, the C core cannot fopen() one, and the
     shelf is a folder of folders with lock files in it. What the picker returns
     is now turned back into the path it stands for; the permission is what
     makes that path openable. See localised() and canUseAnyFolder() in
     StateFileManager.cpp.

     One permission does still appear in the built manifest, and it is not ours
     to remove: androidx.core declares
     "<applicationId>.DYNAMIC_RECEIVER_NOT_EXPORTED_PERMISSION", a signature
     permission scoped to this app that exists so its own broadcast receivers
     are not exported. It grants nothing to the app, is never shown to the user
     and never prompts. Deleting it with tools:node="remove" would only break
     androidx's own receiver registration.

  2. package, and a launcher icon. Qt's default is org.qtproject.example.<app>,
     which would put this calculator in the same namespace as Qt's examples and
     next to a blank icon in the launcher. It is br.gbmaizol.agape48 since
     2026sep07, on Gert's instruction: "Why dk.geeag? Please make a note to
     change it to something more related to me, like br.gbmaizol.agape48." The
     old prefix was invented in the first commit and defended by a comment that
     argued in a circle - the Java was in that package because the same commit
     had put it there. A reverse-DNS prefix names the publisher, and this one
     names him. Renaming is a NEW APP IDENTITY: the build installs beside the
     old one instead of over it, and nothing carries across, which is why it was
     done on the night the old install was being deleted anyway.

  3. android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback is FALSE, and it is the reason the
     back gesture works at all. Android 13 introduced a second way to deliver
     "back": an app that opts in stops receiving KEYCODE_BACK as a key event in
     its view hierarchy and gets an OnBackInvokedCallback instead. Qt 6.12's
     QtActivityBase registers no such callback - it has dispatchKeyEvent,
     onKeyDown and onKeyUp and nothing else - so with the opt-in ON the system
     had nobody to call and did the default thing, which is to finish the
     activity.

     Measured on Gert's phone on 2026sep09, with the Settings page open: back
     put the app in the launcher, every time, and a console.log at the top of
     the QML key handler never printed. With this attribute false the same
     press prints "PROBE key 16777313", which is Qt::Key_Back, and Settings
     closes onto the calculator the way dogfood android-08 line 16 asked.

     So the whole page-and-back design was correct and unreachable. Revisit
     this only when Qt registers a callback of its own; the day it does, this
     attribute becomes the way to get the predictive-back animation.

  4. android:screenOrientation is "portrait", which is the dogfood round's
     answer to the question this comment used to leave open. Gert, 2026sep07,
     on the phone: "please keep the Agape48 from rotating when the phone turns
     around. It looks too ugly." A 48GX is a portrait object - the face is 854
     wide for 1438 tall - so landscape can only letterbox it or stretch it, and
     the Android build already stretches to fill, which in landscape would make
     the keys twice as wide as they are tall.
-->
<manifest xmlns:android="http://schemas.android.com/apk/res/android"
    package="br.gbmaizol.agape48"
    android:installLocation="auto"
    android:versionCode="-- %%INSERT_VERSION_CODE%% --"
    android:versionName="-- %%INSERT_VERSION_NAME%% --">
    <!-- %%INSERT_FEATURES -->
    <uses-permission android:name="android.permission.MANAGE_EXTERNAL_STORAGE" />
    <supports-screens
        android:anyDensity="true"
        android:largeScreens="true"
        android:normalScreens="true"
        android:smallScreens="true" />
    <application
        android:name="org.qtproject.qt.android.bindings.QtApplication"
        android:hardwareAccelerated="true"
        android:label="-- %%INSERT_APP_NAME%% --"
        android:icon="@mipmap/ic_launcher"
        android:roundIcon="@mipmap/ic_launcher_round"
        android:enableOnBackInvokedCallback="false"
        android:allowBackup="true"
        android:fullBackupOnly="false">
        <activity
            android:name="org.qtproject.qt.android.bindings.QtActivity"
            android:configChanges="orientation|uiMode|screenLayout|screenSize|smallestScreenSize|layoutDirection|locale|fontScale|keyboard|keyboardHidden|navigation|mcc|mnc|density"
            android:label="-- %%INSERT_APP_NAME%% --"
            android:launchMode="singleTop"
            android:screenOrientation="portrait"
            android:exported="true">
            <intent-filter>
                <action android:name="android.intent.action.MAIN" />
                <category android:name="android.intent.category.LAUNCHER" />
            </intent-filter>

            <meta-data
                android:name="android.app.lib_name"
                android:value="-- %%INSERT_APP_LIB_NAME%% --" />

            <meta-data
                android:name="android.app.arguments"
                android:value="-- %%INSERT_APP_ARGUMENTS%% --" />
        </activity>

        <provider
            android:name="androidx.core.content.FileProvider"
            android:authorities="${applicationId}.qtprovider"
            android:exported="false"
            android:grantUriPermissions="true">
            <meta-data
                android:name="android.support.FILE_PROVIDER_PATHS"
                android:resource="@xml/qtprovider_paths"/>
        </provider>
    </application>
</manifest>
